{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>And he begot a son and called his name Noah.</b> The reckoning of ten is the foundation of the world and its perpetuation. And behold, Noah was the tenth from Adam, and it was fitting that the world should be perpetuated through him. And since those generations were destined to be blotted out by the Flood, and there was none among them who called on the name of YHWH except Noah alone, therefore you will not find among any of them the mention of 'son' nor the mention of 'name,' until Noah came and Scripture made mention concerning him: <b>and he begot a son and called his name Noah.</b> And the word 'son' (בן) is derived from 'building' (בנין), for the world was built and perpetuated through him. Also, the essence of a thing and its perpetuation is called 'name,' in the manner of what is written (Proverbs 10): ״ושם רשעים ירקב״ — 'and the name of the wicked shall rot' — for the concept of rotting does not apply to a name, since a name is not a physical thing; rather the intent is upon his very self and his essence, that rotting should rule over it.<br><b>And the fact</b> that he called his name Noah (נח) — he should have called him Menachem (מנחם, 'comforter'), according to the matter of which he said: <b>this one shall comfort us</b> (זה ינחמנו). But it is possible to interpret that the mem is absorbed (מובלעת), and the essence of the name is nacham (נחם), in the manner of what is written (Zechariah 11): ״לאחד קראתי נעם ולאחד קראתי חבלים״. For thus is the way of Scripture in many names, to shorten the word. And the reason for the shortening is so that the name Noah (נח) would encompass favor (חן), as it is said: ״ונח מצא חן בעיני ה׳״. And because Noah was tenth, and the tenth is holy to YHWH, therefore Scripture made mention concerning him the word 'this' (זה), in the language of (Exodus 15): ״זה אלי ואנוהו״ — 'This is my El and I will glorify Him.' That is to say: 'this one (זה) shall comfort us' — in the merit of 'this' (זה). And since the word זה embraces these two intentions, two cantillation signs (טעמים) were written on the word זה. And let this matter not be light in your eyes, for the entire Torah is wholly allusions and intellectual matters spoken with perfect providence to whoever contemplates the Book of the Torah of Elohim. And concerning this our Rabbis of blessed memory darshaned that even the cantillation signs (טעמים) in the Torah were given from Sinai, and they proved this from what is written (Nehemiah 8): ״ושום שכל ויבינו במקרא״, and they darshaned thus: ושום שכל — these are the verses; ויבינו במקרא — these are the divisions of the cantillation signs (פסקי טעמים)."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:10:41.887020319Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}